    {
      "question": "e-6190",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "14d34f7ce4fda9e67b207e810900d39bac9b9cfc087c979edda6ed2b01ec4662",
      "new_sha256": "601de9e37e8547e31468476c731ab5cb654738f41187324dc7b12d49f4bc7bb4",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6190",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we can solve for the norm $\\Vert x \\Vert$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016-\\frac{x}{2}\u2016 & =5 \\\\ \u2016-\\frac{1}{2}x\u2016 & =5 \\\\ |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\cdot \u2016x\u2016 & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \u2016x\u2016 & =5 \\\\ \u2016x\u2016 & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $\\Vert 3 \\mathbf{x} \\Vert \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u20163x\u2016 & =3\\cdot \u2016x\u2016 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044632/example-6190.json",
          "file_sha256": "5e08c7b8ec95627ab991f48c98062b1539d85466759ceb6975ae846739a9047a"
